{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact clean pushed head 7e88b9aa3fba6839188af2fdea72737d09a366b5, artifact dee260a5 at spike/querysandbox. Both items of repair request dc0a3726 are delivered, the scope did not widen, and the boundary I was most worried about is intact. One measured gap is recorded below and is not a blocker.\n\nSCOPE, CHECKED BECAUSE I ASKED FOR IT TO BE NARROW. 29 insertions, 12 deletions across README.md, sandbox.go and sandbox_test.go. My request said the authorizer, engine limits, deadline, bounds and every test were approved as they stood and must not move. Nothing else moved. dee260a5 is also the only live artifact at this path - 25e2fa64 was retired rather than left beside it, which is the succession discipline I have spent the afternoon asking other lanes for and did not have to ask for here.\n\nTHE README CORRECTION IS THE RIGHT ONE, not a relocation. The new text names the mechanism - Query materializes a complete row before applying MaxResultBytes - gives the measured figure, gives the roughly 2 MiB ceiling under the existing 32-column and 64 KiB limits, and keeps the Go slice, string, allocator and encoding overhead while subordinating it: \"the smaller ... overhead sits beside that term\". That is exactly the fix. The old sentence was true and named only the small term; this one names the dominant term and keeps the small one in proportion.\n\nI RE-RAN MY OWN MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN TRUSTING THE NUMBER I SUPPLIED. At this head, four columns of 60 KiB: rows returned 0, Truncated true, transient allocation 264,432 bytes, 32.3 times the 8192-byte cap. The README says 264,352. That is an 80-byte difference across two runs, which is run-to-run variance in Go allocator accounting rather than a discrepancy - but it is worth stating that the figure is a measurement with noise rather than a constant, so nobody later treats it as exact or files a defect when their own probe differs in the third digit. The ratio and the order of magnitude are what the sentence actually claims, and both hold.\n\nTHE DECODE FIX IS PINNED AT ITS OWN DECISION SITE. columnValue now returns an error, and splitting columnValueOfType so the datatype is a parameter is a good seam: it lets the unreachable arm be tested without a driver that can produce it, and without mocking a statement. I reverted the default arm to panic and TestColumnValueRejectsUnexpectedSQLiteDatatype fails, panicking as the old code did. Tree byte-clean afterwards.\n\nTHE AUTHORIZER IS UNTOUCHED, VERIFIED BY MUTATION AND NOT BY DIFF. The surest way to lose a boundary is to have it quietly weakened during an unrelated repair, so I re-ran the check from my first review at this head: replacing the default arm with AUTH_OK still kills TestApplicationQueryRefusals on both PRAGMA subtests, TestAuthorizerMutationProof, and TestAuthorizerIsDefaultDeny. Default-deny by construction survives the repair.\n\nTHE ONE GAP, MEASURED. Dropping the error propagation in Query - replacing the three-line early return with a discarded error - leaves the entire suite green. So the refusal is proven at columnValueOfType but nothing proves it reaches the caller; a swallowed decode error would return a wrong value rather than deny, which is the opposite of the principle the change exists to serve.\n\nI am not blocking on it, and the reason is not that it is small. Pinning that wire needs an injectable statement seam in Query for a path unreachable against the pinned driver, and building that machinery into a spike would cost more clarity than the coverage buys. The honest position is that this is structurally evident in three visible lines rather than test-enforced, and a production implementation of this boundary should pin it. Recording it so that decision is made deliberately later rather than discovered.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Unchanged from my first review and re-checked: the spike sits under spike/ beside the seven production layers, changes none of their contracts, and does not import into or teach the kernel. docs/reference/architecture.md needs no update in this head. The gaps list remains a narrowing of a future production contract rather than pressure on the kernel, which is the stop rule request 466a084e carried.\n\nSECURITY. The trust boundary is agent-authored SQL against a projection and it is unchanged: fail-closed at the authorizer, at the connection configuration, and at the statement shape. This head strictly improves one failure mode, replacing a process-killing panic with a refusal in the component whose thesis is that unknown inputs deny. The memory overshoot is now documented rather than misattributed, which is the security-relevant part: a reader sizing a pool from this page is no longer wrong by two orders of magnitude. No unresolved defect.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing to cut. The columnValueOfType split is the smallest change that makes the arm testable, and adding a wrapper rather than threading a datatype through every call site is the right side of that trade.\n\nGATES, run by me from a clean detached checkout at the exact head, worktree byte-clean after all three mutations were reverted: gofmt silent, git diff --check clean, go build ./... and go vet ./... exit 0, go test ./spike/querysandbox -count=10 exit 0, go test -race ./spike/querysandbox -count=1 exit 0, go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with 33 packages ok.\n\nCodex may merge this exact head once this verdict is ratified. Any change to the head returns it to review.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:dee260a595e29531718c1f58976d35fa515de84c","head":"7e88b9aa3fba6839188af2fdea72737d09a366b5","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:dbaaade52c7fd9017ddff089df754aab920137ee\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:dbaaade52c7fd9017ddff089df754aab920137ee","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}